@meta
id: 24-05
title: Move lines to a mark
chapter: 24
type: lesson
skills: ex-move
par: 28
minutes: 5
vscode: cut, scroll, find the section, paste
open: CHANGELOG.md

@brief
Two entries under `Unreleased`, lines 6 and 7, actually went out in
1.4.0. They belong at the end of the 1.4.0 list, directly after the
line about email receipts. The list order matters and nothing else in
the file changes.

The 1.4.0 section is a long way down and you should not have to know
its line number. Find the destination with a search, drop a mark on it,
come back, and move the lines to the mark. The destination of `m` is an
address, and a mark is an address.

@keys
| `/receipts` | find the last line of the 1.4.0 list |
| `ma` | mark it |
| `:6,7m'a` | move lines 6 to 7 to below mark `a` |

@hints
The destination is the line the moved text lands after, so mark the
last entry of the 1.4.0 list, not the heading.
--
Anywhere you would write a line number in a range or a destination, a
mark works: `'a`. You used `:'a,'bs` in chapter 14; this is the same
thing on the other side of the command.
--
`/receipts` Enter, `ma`, then `:6,7m'a` and Enter. You do not need to
go back to line 6 first; the range says where the lines are.

@solution
    /receipts<Enter>  ma  :6,7m'a<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Twenty-four keystrokes, and the only number you typed was the one you
could read off the screen.

The mark is doing the job a line number would have done if you had
been willing to scroll and read it. Searching for the destination and
marking it is faster and, more importantly, does not go wrong when the
file is longer than the screen. It is also the shape that scales: mark
a destination once, and move as many things to it as you like from
wherever you find them.

Marks survive the move. After `:6,7m'a`, mark `a` is still on the
receipts line, now two lines higher than it was. If you had a third
entry to send the same way, `:m'a` would put it above the two you just
moved, because the mark is on the line *before* them. Mark the last
moved line instead if order matters.

@start CHANGELOG.md
# Changelog

## Unreleased

- Add partial refunds
- Fix duplicate charge on retried checkout
- Record cancellation reason on orders
- Add customer tiers

## 1.4.0

- Add refund window
- Retry gateway calls with backoff
- Add order notes to the admin view
- Expose `placedAt` on the order resolver
- Reject cancellation of shipped orders
- Log the payment reference on refund
- Add `deleted` to every repository filter
- Send email receipts

## 1.3.0

- Add customer lookup by email
- Return 404 for unknown orders
- Add the payment service
- Add the email service
- Register services in the DI container

## 1.2.0

- Add order cancellation
- Add order totals
- Add the customer entity

## 1.1.0

- Add the order entity
- Add the order repository
- Add the order provider
- Add the order resolver

## 1.0.0

- Initial release

